  "id": "Compound::DB04845 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Ixabepilone is implicated in the treatment of breast cancer through its inclusion in the Microtubule Inhibition pathway, which includes both Eribulin and Paclitaxel. These compounds are known to inhibit microtubules, a critical process in cell division, and thus are used in the treatment of various cancers, including breast cancer. The shared lowest common ancestor of Ixabepilone, Eribulin, and Paclitaxel is the Organic Molecular Entity, suggesting a common mechanism of action in the context of microtubule inhibition. This pathway highlights the role of microtubule-targeting agents in breast cancer therapy, with Ixabepilone potentially contributing through its inclusion in the same class of drugs. The evidence suggests that Ixabepilone may exert its therapeutic effect through a mechanism that involves microtubule inhibition, aligning it with other agents in this class. The relationship between Ixabepilone and breast cancer is thus supported by its classification within a pathway that targets microtubules, which are essential for cell division and are a known target in cancer treatment.",
